  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Giant bronze masks were smashed, burned, then buried. Guanghan, in Sichuan, 1986. Workers cutting a ditch hit two pits packed with ruined treasure.",
        "visual_query": "sichuan bronze age excavation",
        "visual_fallback": "excavation pit"
      },
      {
        "narration": "One pit alone held around eight hundred objects. Bronze heads with almond eyes and pupils that bulge straight out. Gold foil. Ivory. A standing figure two and a half metres tall.",
        "visual_query": "ancient chinese bronze mask",
        "visual_fallback": "bronze artifacts"
      },
      {
        "narration": "And not one written word. Nothing. A civilization good enough to cast all of this left no records to explain any of it.",
        "visual_query": "sichuan river valley mist",
        "visual_fallback": "misty valley"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Then from 2020 six more pits opened up, one holding a gold mask: 280 grams, about 84 percent pure.",
        "visual_query": "chinese gold mask artifact",
        "visual_fallback": "gold mask"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The likeliest answer is the lost kingdom of Shu, breaking its own sacred objects on purpose before burying them.",
        "visual_query": "ancient sichuan temple ruins",
        "visual_fallback": "ancient china"
      }
    ],
